/* EXAMS — weekly (1–3) + final (4). Filled by the content run; keep {} valid
   so the map renders during authoring. Same shape as level-ru-c1.js EXAMS. */
const EXAMS = {"1":{"week":1,"title":"Exam · Week 1","intro":"Six days, one idea from six angles: at C2 a word isn't merely correct, or even appropriate — it's exact, and it carries a shade beyond the dictionary. These 25 questions pull from all seven days and ask, of every near-synonym, not «is it right?» but «what does it carry?». Clear 80% and Week 2 opens; if a couple slip, glance back at that day and run it again — no rush.","review":["<b>The one exact word</b> — from a family that all «fit», one carries the shade you mean: only <b>плести́сь</b> is «walk» plus exhaustion; only <b>про́мах</b> is a misfired decision (vs <b>огре́х</b> a craft flaw, <b>опло́шность</b> a careless slip). Ask what a word <b>carries</b>, not just what it means.","<b>Connotation</b> — a word smuggles in a verdict. Same fact, opposite charge: <b>упо́рный</b> praises, <b>упря́мый</b> complains; <b>бережли́вый</b> admires, <b>скупо́й</b> sneers. And <b>пресло́вутый</b> isn't «famous» — it's «notorious, overhyped». Pick the charged word by accident and you leak an opinion.","<b>Collocation</b> (<b>сочета́емость</b>) — words keep fixed company. You <b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b> (never «сде́лать»), <b>одержа́ть побе́ду</b>, <b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b>; rain is <b>проливно́й</b>, eyes are <b>ка́рие</b>. Never cross <b>игра́ть роль</b> with <b>име́ть значе́ние</b>.","<b>Expressive word-building</b> — Russian folds a whole English clause into one built word: <b>зачита́ться</b> (get lost in reading), <b>недосоли́ть</b> (put in too little salt), <b>перемудри́ть</b> (overthink it and mess it up). Meet a new one? <b>Parse, don't panic</b> — root plus affixes.","<b>The finest grain of aspect</b> (<b>спо́собы де́йствия</b>) — a prefix tunes not the meaning but the grain: delimitative <b>по-</b> (<b>посиде́ть</b>, a cozy little while), inceptive <b>за-</b> (<b>запе́ть</b>, burst into song), saturative <b>на-…-ся</b> (<b>наговори́ться</b>, talk one's fill).","<b>Last-shade particles</b> — a thin film of attitude on a complete sentence: <b>уж</b> presses or softens, <b>-та́ки</b> means «after all, despite it», <b>аж</b> marks a jolt, <b>небо́сь</b> a warm «I bet», <b>во́все не</b> sharpens a negation. The whole craft is the <b>dose</b> — one breathes, three make a caricature.","The one question under all of it: not <b>ве́рно ли</b> (is it correct), not even <b>уме́стно ли</b> (is it appropriate), but <b>то ли э́то са́мое сло́во</b> — is this the very word a native would have chosen?"],"q":[{"type":"match","q":"Match each verb of walking to the picture it carries — the shade, not the register (all four are neutral, standard Russian).","pairs":[["шага́ть","stride — big, deliberate, purposeful steps"],["плести́сь","trudge — drag yourself along, slow and weary"],["семени́ть","mince — tiny, quick steps"],["брести́","plod, wander — slow and aimless, worn out"]],"hint":"Ask what each verb pictures — how the feet move, how the body feels — not how formal it is 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"A wrong strategic move — an error of judgment — that cost the company its market. The exact noun is…","opts":["огре́х","опло́шность","про́мах"],"answer":"про́мах","why":"A blunder of judgment → <b>про́мах</b> (like a shot that misses). <b>огре́х</b> is a rough spot left by careless work; <b>опло́шность</b> is a small careless slip — right family, wrong shade.","hint":"Not a craftsman's flaw, not a careless slip — a misfired decision 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In a warm reference you want to praise a colleague who never backs down — with clear approval. Choose:","opts":["упря́мый","упо́рный","самоуве́ренный"],"answer":"упо́рный","why":"<b>упо́рный</b> (persistent) carries warm approval. <b>упря́мый</b> (stubborn) and <b>самоуве́ренный</b> (cocky) judge the very same behaviour badly — same fact, opposite verdict.","hint":"All three fit the behaviour; only one flatters instead of sneering 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Опя́ть э́та пресло́вутая рефо́рма — все от неё уста́ли.","accept":["that notorious reform again — everyone's sick of it","that notorious reform again, everyone is sick of it","again that notorious reform — everyone's tired of it","that overhyped reform again — everyone's sick of it","that notorious reform again — everybody is tired of it"],"model":"That notorious reform again — everyone's sick of it.","hint":"«пресло́вутый» isn't «famous» — it drips scorn: notorious, overhyped 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"You want to call a plan overhyped — the «so-called» one nobody really believes in. The word that already carries that scorn is…","opts":["знамени́тый","изве́стный","пресло́вутый"],"answer":"пресло́вутый","why":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> arrives pre-loaded with contempt — «that overhyped one». <b>знамени́тый</b> (renowned) and <b>изве́стный</b> (well-known) are neutral-to-positive, with no sneer.","hint":"Two are plain «famous»; one has the eye-roll built in 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он ___ си́льное влия́ние на весь прое́кт.» — which verb keeps company with «влия́ние»?","opts":["сде́лал","оказа́л","дал"],"answer":"оказа́л","why":"«влия́ние» marries <b>оказа́ть</b> → оказа́л. «сде́лать влия́ние» calques English «make»; «дать влия́ние» is no better — every word is correct, only the partnership is broken.","hint":"Influence isn't «made» in Russian — recall the fixed pair 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"First aid was given to the victim — use the verb that keeps company with «по́мощь»: «Пострада́вшему ___ пе́рвую по́мощь.» <i>(past tense)</i>","accept":["оказали","оказал","оказала"],"model":"оказа́ли","hint":"Help keeps company with «оказа́ть», never «сде́лать» or «дать» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́тот фа́ктор ___ большо́е значе́ние.» — which verb keeps company with «значе́ние»?","opts":["име́ет","игра́ет","де́лает"],"answer":"име́ет","why":"Russian fixes <b>име́ть значе́ние</b> and <b>игра́ть роль</b> — and never crosses them. «игра́ет значе́ние» is the famous mix-up; «де́лает» is the English calque.","hint":"«значе́ние» you <i>have</i>; a «роль» you <i>play</i> — don't cross the wires 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"The team won a decisive victory in the final.","accept":["команда одержала решительную победу в финале","команда одержала решительную победу в финале.","команда одержала уверенную победу в финале","команда одержала победу в финале"],"model":"Кома́нда одержа́ла реши́тельную побе́ду в фина́ле.","hint":"A victory is «gained», not «made» — reach for «одержа́ть побе́ду» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Не ___ её — она́ сильне́е, чем ка́жется.» — which built verb means «to underestimate» (недо- = too little)?","opts":["переоце́нивай","недооце́нивай","оце́нивай"],"answer":"недооце́нивай","why":"<b>недо-</b> «too little» → <b>недооце́нивать</b> (to underrate). <b>переоце́нивать</b> is the opposite (over-value); <b>оце́нивать</b> is plain «to value». The prefix is the whole verdict.","hint":"The prefix says «too little» — pick the one that undershoots 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"One built verb for «to put in too little salt» (prefix недо- glued on): «Ка́жется, я ___ суп — совсе́м пре́сный.»","accept":["недосолил"],"model":"недосоли́л","hint":"One word, not «не» plus a verb: недо- plus соли́ть folds «too little» right in 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Он так зачита́лся, что забы́л о вре́мени.","accept":["he got so lost in reading that he forgot the time","he got so absorbed in reading he forgot about the time","he got so engrossed in the book he lost track of time","he was so lost in reading he forgot the time","he got so caught up in reading he forgot the time"],"model":"He got so lost in reading that he forgot the time.","hint":"<b>зачита́ться</b> folds a whole clause into one verb — за-…-ся = getting absorbed 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«По́сле шу́тки все ра́зом ___.» — the sudden onset, «burst out talking» (inceptive за-):","opts":["заговори́ли","поговори́ли","наговори́лись"],"answer":"заговори́ли","why":"Inceptive <b>за-</b> is the switch flipping on → <b>заговори́ли</b> (started talking all at once). <b>поговори́ли</b> is «chatted a while» (по-); <b>наговори́лись</b> is «talked their fill» (на-…-ся).","hint":"Which prefix marks the very moment it kicks in? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Мы сла́вно ___ часо́к и разошли́сь.» — a pleasant, bounded little while (delimitative по-):","opts":["посиде́ли","засиде́лись","просиде́ли"],"answer":"посиде́ли","why":"Delimitative <b>по-</b> = a little, and pleasantly → <b>посиде́ли</b>. <b>засиде́лись</b> = stayed too long (overstayed); <b>просиде́ли</b> = sat through the whole time. Same root, different grain.","hint":"«Сла́вно… часо́к» wants the cozy, capped little while, not «stayed too long» 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Delimitative по- for a cozy little while: «Дава́й ___ здесь мину́т де́сять, отдохнём.» <i>(from сиде́ть)</i>","accept":["посидим"],"model":"посиди́м","hint":"Add по- to «сиде́ть» to cap the time and warm the tone 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"The sisters hadn't seen each other for a year and talked their fill till dawn.","accept":["сёстры не виделись год и наговорились до рассвета","сестры не виделись год и наговорились до рассвета","сёстры не виделись целый год и наговорились до рассвета","сёстры год не виделись и наговорились до рассвета"],"model":"Сёстры не ви́делись год и наговори́лись до рассве́та.","hint":"«talked their fill» is the saturative на-…-ся → наговори́лись 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«От неожи́данности она́ ___ всплесну́ла рука́ми.» — the jolt, a reaction so strong it surprises her:","opts":["аж","уж","небо́сь"],"answer":"аж","why":"<b>аж</b> marks a jolt — «even to that point». <b>уж</b> presses or softens the word beside it; <b>небо́сь</b> is a warm «I bet» guess — neither is a jolt.","hint":"Which particle carries «even to that point», the far edge of a reaction? 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"The emphatic particle that sharpens a negation to «not … at all»: «Э́то ___ не тру́дно, че́стно.»","accept":["вовсе"],"model":"во́все","hint":"It pushes «не» past a plain negation — «not at all» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Доро́га была́ тру́дной, но он ___.» — «after all, in the end, despite everything»:","opts":["уже́ добра́лся","то́лько добра́лся","добра́лся-та́ки"],"answer":"добра́лся-та́ки","why":"«after all, despite the hard road» → <b>добра́лся-та́ки</b> (-та́ки hung on the verb). «уже́ добра́лся» is just «already»; «то́лько добра́лся» is «only just now».","hint":"The shade is «expectation said no, reality said yes» — that's «-та́ки», not «уже́» 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble a resigned, gentle apology: «Do forgive me — I honestly forgot.»","answer":"уж прости я и вправду забыл","accept":["уж прости я и вправду забыл","уж прости, я и вправду забыл","уж прости — я и вправду забыл"],"hint":"«уж» presses gently on «прости́», turning a bare command into a sigh 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"He wasn't walking — he was trudging, barely lifting his feet from exhaustion.","accept":["он не шёл а плёлся едва поднимая ноги от усталости","он не шёл, а плёлся, едва поднимая ноги от усталости","он не шел а плелся едва поднимая ноги от усталости","он не шел, а плелся, едва поднимая ноги от усталости","он не шёл, а плёлся, еле поднимая ноги от усталости"],"model":"Он не шёл, а плёлся, едва́ поднима́я но́ги от уста́лости.","hint":"«barely alive with fatigue» is the exact verb «плести́сь», not the flat «идти́» 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"Take one neutral fact about a person — how they spend money, or how firmly they hold a decision — and describe them in Russian TWICE, changing only the key word: once with the ADMIRING connotation (бережли́вый, упо́рный, уве́ренный), once with the CONTEMPTUOUS twin for the very same fact (скупо́й, упря́мый, самоуве́ренный). Then name, in a word or two, the verdict each choice smuggles in.","keywords":["бережливый","скупой","упорный","упрямый","коннотация","оценка"],"model":"Оце́нка со зна́ком плюс: О́льга упо́рная — она́ не отступа́ет, пока́ не добьётся своего́. Оце́нка со зна́ком ми́нус: О́льга упря́мая — с ней невозмо́жно договори́ться. Фа́кт оди́н, но «упо́рная» хва́лит, а «упря́мая» осужда́ет — вся ра́зница в коннота́ции.","minWords":20,"hint":"The facts never change — only the charge does. The listener hears the verdict the word carries, not the one in your head 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each word to the C2 idea it demonstrates — one thread from each day of the week.","pairs":[["плести́сь","the one exact word — «walk» plus exhaustion (day 1)"],["пресло́вутый","connotation — a built-in sneer (day 2)"],["оказа́ть влия́ние","collocation — words that keep company (day 3)"],["посиде́ть","the finest grain of aspect — a cozy little while (day 5)"]],"hint":"Sort them by the lesson each shows off: exact word, hidden charge, fixed pair, aspect shade 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble a line joining the fixed collocation with an «after all» particle: «The team won the victory after all, despite everything.»","answer":"команда одержала-таки победу вопреки всему","accept":["команда одержала-таки победу вопреки всему","команда таки одержала победу вопреки всему","команда одержала таки победу вопреки всему"],"hint":"«одержа́ть побе́ду» is the fixed pair; hang «-та́ки» on the verb for «after all» 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"Write 4–5 Russian sentences about ONE small moment — someone arriving, someone reading, a rainy evening — and pack this week's precision into it: reach for the ONE exact verb (not the flat default), one word with the right connotation, one true collocation (e.g. проливно́й до́ждь, оказа́ть по́мощь), and exactly ONE last-shade particle. Then, in a line, point to each choice and name what it carries — without over-salting.","keywords":["проливной","плелась","зачиталась","оказать","таки","точное слово"],"model":"Проливно́й дождь лил весь ве́чер, и Ле́на не шла домо́й, а плела́сь. Она́ так зачита́лась в метро́, что чуть не прое́хала свою́ ста́нцию. «Уж э́ти кни́ги», — вздохну́ла она́, но кни́гу-та́ки не закры́ла. Здесь ка́ждое сло́во то́чное: «проливно́й» — коллока́ция, «плела́сь» — уста́лость, «зачита́лась» — це́лая мысль в одно́м сло́ве, а «-та́ки» — оди́н то́чный отте́нок, и бо́льше ни одно́й ли́шней части́цы.","minWords":30,"hint":"The proof of the week is one natural paragraph where every word is the exact one — and where you stopped at ONE particle 🙂"}]},"2":{"week":2,"title":"Exam · Week 2","intro":"Week two was about meaning that hides — under the words, against them, inside them, or pointing somewhere else. These 25 questions ask the C2 question: not «what does the line say?» but «what does it mean, and where does it point?». Clear 80% and Week 3 opens; miss a few and it's worth a glance back at the day before you run it again.","review":["<b>Подте́кст</b> (subtext) — the meaning under the words. The small carriers of a barb: <b>про́сто</b>, <b>коне́чно</b>, <b>да нет</b>, <b>интере́сно, кто…</b>. Answer the reproach, not the surface — but don't invent a hint in every neutral line.","<b>Иро́ния и сарка́зм</b> — <b>антифра́зис</b>, a word standing for its own opposite. Ready-made sneers <b>то́же мне</b>, <b>как же!</b>, <b>ну-ну</b>, <b>спаси́бо, удружи́л</b>; irony's marks are a flat tone and ironic «quotes».","<b>Языкова́я игра́</b> — the <b>каламбу́р</b> (two senses in one form: <b>ключ</b>, <b>язы́к</b>), paronyms pulled together (<b>служи́ть / прислу́живаться</b>), <b>буквализа́ция</b> and <b>контамина́ция</b>. It lives in the sounds — and barely survives translation.","<b>Прецеде́нтные фено́мены</b> — name / phrase / text / situation: <b>Плю́шкин</b>, «<b>А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест</b>», «<b>Го́ре от ума́</b>», «<b>А коро́ль-то го́лый</b>». The meaning is the source, not the literal words — and never misquote.","The wells everyone drinks from — <b>Крыло́в</b>, <b>Грибое́дов</b>, <b>Толсто́й</b>, <b>Булга́ков</b>, cinema, songs, fairy tales: «<b>Рукопи́си не горя́т</b>», «<b>Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи…</b>», «<b>Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?</b>».","<b>Стилиза́ция</b> — wearing a genre on purpose: «<b>Жил-был…</b>» (ска́зка), «<b>В ле́то…</b>» (ле́топись), <b>канцеляри́т</b>, the invented mask <b>Козьма́ Прутко́в</b>. Held whole it's art; leaked by accident it's a <b>срыв сти́ля</b>.","<b>Анекдо́т</b> — <b>зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн</b>, the funny word last. Engines: pun, grammar, absurd, a cultural detail; the cast <b>Шти́рлиц</b>, <b>Во́вочка</b>, <b>чу́кча</b>; and <b>самоиро́ния</b>, laughing at oneself.","The one question all week: not «what do the words say?» but «what is meant <b>under</b> or <b>against</b> them — and which source do they point to?»."],"q":[{"type":"choice","q":"A friend, mid-argument, says «Спаси́бо, я сам» in a clipped, flat voice. Under the words, they mean —","opts":["a warm thank-you for the offer","a request to be helped after all","«back off — I don't want your help»"],"answer":"«back off — I don't want your help»","why":"Clipped and flat, «Спаси́бо, я сам» isn't gratitude — the subtext is «leave me to it». A native answers the rebuff, not the thanks.","hint":"A flat, clipped «thanks» in a tense moment rarely means thanks — trust the tone 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Мо́жешь не спеши́ть», said to a colleague who is famously, chronically late, most likely means —","opts":["a jab about their lateness","genuine permission to take their time","an instruction to hurry up"],"answer":"a jab about their lateness","why":"Kind words, sore situation → trust the situation. To the always-late, «you needn't rush» is a dig, not a favour.","hint":"Sunny words on a sore spot — the situation decides, not the dictionary 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Complete the idiom for hearing the unspoken meaning: «чита́ть ме́жду ___». <i>(one Russian noun)</i>","accept":["строк"],"model":"строк","hint":"To read between the… — where the unprinted meaning lives 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Я про́сто спра́шиваю.","accept":["i'm just asking","im just asking","i am just asking","i'm only asking","i am only asking","im only asking"],"model":"I'm just asking.","hint":"Translate the surface — but remember the subtext underneath is usually a reproach 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Someone knocks a full cup onto the floor and hears a flat, toneless «Ну ты молоде́ц». The line is —","opts":["sincere praise for their skill","irony — it means the opposite (antiphrasis)","a neutral, factual remark"],"answer":"irony — it means the opposite (antiphrasis)","why":"Sunny word, grim moment, flat tone → антифра́зис: «молоде́ц» carries its own reverse. Believe the moment, not the word.","hint":"Broken cup + flat «well done» — which reading flips the word to its opposite? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он изви́нится? Как же!» — the ready-made «Как же!» tells you the speaker is sure he —","opts":["certainly will apologize","has already apologized","certainly will not apologize"],"answer":"certainly will not apologize","why":"«Как же!» is a flat «yeah, right» — a lexical sneer that denies what was just said. He won't.","hint":"«Как же!» = «yeah, right» — it dismisses the idea, whatever the tone 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"The ready-made sneer that deflates the word after it: «___ мне геро́й!» = «some hero you are». <i>(one Russian word)</i>","accept":["тоже"],"model":"то́же","hint":"A two-word opener that mocks whatever noun follows it 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"He'll pay it back? Yeah, right!","accept":["он вернёт деньги? как же!","он вернет деньги? как же!","он вернёт долг? как же!","он вернет долг? как же!","он отдаст деньги? как же!","он отдаст долг? как же!","он вернёт? как же!","он вернет? как же!"],"model":"Он вернёт де́ньги? Как же!","hint":"Reach for the ready-made ironic «Как же!» to carry «yeah, right» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"A shop-sign pun leans on one word carrying two live senses at once. Which word can anchor it?","opts":["ключ (a key / a spring)","стул (a chair)","окно́ (a window)"],"answer":"ключ (a key / a spring)","why":"A каламбу́р needs one form holding two meanings together; «ключ» is both a key and a spring, so both fire at once.","hint":"A pun needs a word that lives two lives at the same instant 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Fusing two words into one impossible comic word («Ме́бель без пробле́бель») is called ___. <i>(one Russian noun)</i>","accept":["контаминация"],"model":"контамина́ция","hint":"«Ме́бель» + «без пробле́м» welded into one — the blend has a bookish name 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each single Russian word to the two everyday senses it can hold at once — the raw material of a pun.","pairs":[["ключ","a key / a spring of water"],["язы́к","a tongue / a language"],["лук","an onion / a bow (for arrows)"],["коса́","a braid / a scythe"],["мир","peace / the world"]],"hint":"Each is one form with two live meanings — that double life is what a каламбу́р exploits 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"In 2–3 Russian sentences, explain the play in «Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно» (Грибое́дов): which two near-twin words are set against each other, and how their meanings differ.","keywords":["служить","прислуживаться","пароним","смысл"],"model":"Здесь игра́ет паро́ним: «служи́ть» зна́чит че́стно де́лать де́ло, а «прислу́живаться» — угожда́ть и льстить. Слова́ почти́ одина́ковы по зву́ку, но противополо́жны по смы́слу — в э́том и вся острота́.","minWords":15,"hint":"Name the near-twin pair (паро́ним) and contrast honest service with grovelling 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Call a hoarding, penny-pinching acquaintance a «Плю́шкин» and you're quoting —","opts":["Гончаро́в, «Обло́мов» — a sluggard","Го́голь, «Мёртвые ду́ши» — a miser","Пу́шкин, «Евге́ний Оне́гин» — a dandy"],"answer":"Го́голь, «Мёртвые ду́ши» — a miser","why":"Плю́шкин is Го́голь's hoarder in «Мёртвые ду́ши» — a precedent name that says «miser» with a whole novel behind it.","hint":"The name means «miser» — whose novel does the hoarder come from? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи друг на дру́га…» — where does this famous line actually come from?","opts":["a folk proverb of no fixed author","Че́хов, «Три сестры́»","Толсто́й, «А́нна Каре́нина» (the opening line)"],"answer":"Толсто́й, «А́нна Каре́нина» (the opening line)","why":"It feels folk-anonymous, but it's the exact first line of Толсто́й's «А́нна Каре́нина». Filing a signed line under «proverb» loses its author.","hint":"It sounds like folk wisdom, but a named novelist wrote it to open a book 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"Manuscripts don't burn.","accept":["рукописи не горят"],"model":"Рукопи́си не горя́т.","hint":"Булга́ков's four words on the survival of truth — «manuscripts don't…» 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble Krylov's winged line about someone who ignores you and calmly carries right on: “And Vaska listens on — and eats.”","answer":"а васька слушает да ест","accept":["а васька слушает да ест","а васька слушает, да ест"],"hint":"Krylov's cat: he listens… and keeps right on eating 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"In 2–3 Russian sentences, use the winged phrase «А воз и ны́не там» about a project that never moves, then name its source (the author and the kind of text).","keywords":["воз","ныне","крылов","басня"],"model":"Прое́кт обсужда́ют уже́ це́лый год, а воз и ны́не там — ничего́ не сдви́нулось. Э́то крыла́тая фра́за из ба́сни Крыло́ва «Ле́бедь, Щу́ка и Рак».","minWords":15,"hint":"Drop the Крыло́в line onto a stalled project, then name the fable it comes from 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"A modern business email suddenly ends «…и жи́ли они́ до́лго и сча́стливо». Since it wasn't done on purpose or held throughout, this is —","opts":["стилиза́ция — a deliberate borrowed voice","срыв сти́ля — an accidental slip of register","каламбу́р — a pun"],"answer":"срыв сти́ля — an accidental slip of register","why":"Stylization is deliberate and consistent; a fairy-tale ending leaking into a real email is срыв сти́ля — a register slip the reader hears as a mistake.","hint":"On purpose and held whole = art; leaked by accident = the fault 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Bureaucratic officialese, worn on purpose for deadpan comedy, is ___. <i>(one Russian noun)</i>","accept":["канцелярит"],"model":"канцеляри́т","hint":"The grey language of forms and reports, put on knowingly for a laugh 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"Once upon a time, in a faraway kingdom, there lived a king.","accept":["в некотором царстве, в тридевятом государстве жил-был царь","в некотором царстве в тридевятом государстве жил-был царь","в тридевятом царстве жил-был царь","жил-был в тридевятом царстве царь","жил-был царь в тридевятом царстве","в некотором царстве жил-был царь"],"model":"В не́котором ца́рстве, в тридевя́том госуда́рстве жил-был царь.","hint":"Open with the fairy-tale kit — «Жил-был…» and «в тридевя́том ца́рстве» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю. Слепа́я упа́ла.» What makes this joke work?","opts":["a pun — «вслепу́ю» heard as «в слепу́ю»","a historical fact about the war","pure absurdity, with no wordplay"],"answer":"a pun — «вслепу́ю» heard as «в слепу́ю»","why":"One string of sounds, two parsings: «вслепу́ю» (blindly) vs «в слепу́ю» (at the blind woman). A каламбу́р delivered as a punchline.","hint":"Say «вслепу́ю» aloud twice — where does the second meaning hide? 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"«Как дела́?» — «Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует.»","accept":["how are you? better than anyone, but nobody envies me","how are you? better than everyone, but no one envies me","how are you? better than anyone but nobody envies me","how are things? better than everyone, but nobody envies me","how's it going? better than anyone, but no one envies me","how are you doing? better than everyone, but nobody envies me"],"model":"«How are you?» — «Better than anyone — but nobody envies me.»","hint":"This is самоиро́ния, not a complaint — keep the wry self-mockery in your English 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ме́бель без пробле́бель» (a furniture-shop slogan) and «Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно» both belong to which of the week's moves?","opts":["подте́кст (subtext)","языкова́я игра́ (wordplay)","стилиза́ция (stylization)"],"answer":"языкова́я игра́ (wordplay)","why":"A blend (контамина́ция) and a paronym pair are both языкова́я игра́ — the play splits meaning inside the words themselves, not under or against them.","hint":"Both jokes live inside the words' own sounds and forms — name that family 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each line to the week-two move it uses.","pairs":[["Я про́сто спра́шиваю.","subtext — a reproach under an innocent question"],["Ну ты молоде́ц… (над разби́той ча́шкой)","irony — the word means its opposite"],["Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю.","a pun — one sound, two meanings"],["Рукопи́си не горя́т.","allusion — a phrase pointing to its source"],["Жил-был на све́те старта́п…","stylization — a borrowed genre voice"]],"hint":"Under the words / against the words / inside the words / pointing to a source / wearing a genre 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble the native reply to the reproach hidden under «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» — answering the hurt, not the question: “Don't be cross, I wasn't hiding anything.”","answer":"не сердись, я ничего не скрывал","accept":["не сердись, я ничего не скрывал","не сердись я ничего не скрывал"],"hint":"Answer the reproach, not the surface question — start with «не серди́сь…» 🙂"}]},"3":{"week":3,"title":"Exam · Week 3","intro":"This was the week you stopped being correct and started being able to move a Russian reader. Twenty-five questions gather it all — figures you build rather than name, a speech that climbs, an argument won by conceding first, a claim hedged to exactly the doubt you feel, a sixty-word period held in one arch, and words no English can carry. Clear 80% and Week 4 opens; if a couple slip, glance back at that day and take another run — no rush.","review":["<b>Figures as tools, not labels</b> — you don't spot them, you build them: <b>ана́фора</b> (the same opening repeated), <b>града́ция</b> (each step heavier, the peak last — «пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л»), <b>антите́за</b> (opposites pinned level), <b>хиа́зм</b> (the order crosses, A-B → B-A), the <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b> (the asking is the claim). Day 15.","<b>The dose is the C2 signature</b> — one built figure at the turn lifts a paragraph; three piled per line curdle into purple prose. Restraint, not the pile. Day 15.","<b>The speech is an arch</b> — <b>зачи́н → те́зис → аргуме́нты → кульмина́ция → концо́вка</b>, ordered to climb. It stands on <b>ло́гос</b> (the argument), <b>э́тос</b> (the speaker's standing), and <b>па́фос</b> — elevated, sincere feeling, NOT English «pathos» as pity. Pull the hall in with <b>обраще́ние</b> and the inclusive «мы». Day 16.","<b>Win gracefully</b> — the firmer the «нет», the more courteous its form: <b>«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»</b>. Use <b>усту́пка</b> (grant the strong point, then pivot on «но»), argue <b>по существу́</b> not <b>перехо́д на ли́чности</b>, and name the <b>эристи́ческие уло́вки</b> (подме́на те́зиса, ло́жная диле́мма) rather than throw them. Day 17.","<b>Hedge to the exact doubt</b> — not the blunt «доказано», not a fog of «мо́жет быть», but one calibrated formula: <b>вряд ли</b> (a soft no) · <b>не исключено́</b> (door ajar) · <b>по всей ви́димости</b> (nearly sure, on the evidence) · <b>скло́нен полага́ть</b> (a considered lean). And <b>я́кобы</b> carries doubt — it reports a claim and refuses to vouch for it. Day 18.","<b>The пери́од</b> — a long sentence built as an arch: parallel clauses mount (<b>нагнета́ние</b>), the main clause resolves (<b>разреше́ние</b>). Word order carries focus: <b>те́ма</b> (given) opens, <b>ре́ма</b> (new) lands last, in the strongest position. Long means held, not tangled. Day 19.","<b>Безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</b> — words with no one-word twin: <b>тоска́</b> (an aching, objectless longing, not «sadness»), <b>по́шлость</b> (smug triteness posing as taste — not <b>гру́бость</b>), <b>аво́сь</b> (trust in blind luck), <b>интеллиге́нт</b> (the conscientious, educated class — NOT «intelligent»). You unpack them, never flatten. Day 20.","The one question under all of it: not «is this correct» but «does this carry exactly what I mean» — the figure, the hedge, the concession, the word. Day 21."],"q":[{"type":"choice","q":"Name the figure: «Не мир для нас, а мы для ми́ра». The two halves say the same words with the order reversed.","opts":["хиа́зм — the order crosses (A-B → B-A)","ана́фора — the same opening repeated","града́ция — a mounting climb"],"answer":"хиа́зм — the order crosses (A-B → B-A)","why":"«мир — нас» flips to «мы — ми́ра»: the order crosses over, so it's хиа́зм. Anaphora repeats an opening; gradation climbs.","hint":"Watch the two nouns swap places from the first half to the second — that crossing names the figure 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Three built figures crammed into one line, piled on for their own sake, read as —","opts":["the only correct way to write at C2","purple prose — performed eloquence, not real force","mastery of the toolbox"],"answer":"purple prose — performed eloquence, not real force","why":"The dose rules: one figure at the turn lifts a paragraph; three per line curdle. Restraint is the C2 signature.","hint":"A built figure is a spice, not the whole dish — what happens when you pour in three at once? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In a Russian speech, «па́фос» means —","opts":["pity — something sad and small","elevated, sincere feeling — conviction","dry statistics with no feeling"],"answer":"elevated, sincere feeling — conviction","why":"False friend: English «pathos» drifted toward pity, but Russian па́фос is high, honest feeling — only «дешёвый па́фос» carries the sneer.","hint":"The English cognate has slid toward «pity» — the Russian word points the opposite way, upward 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"The зачи́н — the opening move of a Russian speech — should —","opts":["announce your name and topic first","summarise the conclusion up front","hook the room: a question, an image, a small story"],"answer":"hook the room: a question, an image, a small story","why":"The зачи́н earns attention; «Здра́вствуйте, сего́дня я хочу́ рассказа́ть…» surrenders the room in the first breath.","hint":"Announcing yourself spends the room's attention; the зачи́n is meant to win it 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» is prized in Russian debate because it is —","opts":["vague and non-committal","openly aggressive","firm in substance, courteous in form"],"answer":"firm in substance, courteous in form","why":"It disagrees fully yet asks leave to — steel wrapped in silk, the third road between spineless mush and a rude chop.","hint":"The paradox of Russian argument: the firmer the «нет», the more polished its frame 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«И́ли вы с на́ми, и́ли вы про́тив нас» — which эристи́ческая уло́вка (debate trick) is this?","opts":["ло́жная диле́мма — a false choice","подме́на те́зиса — a straw man","усту́пка — a concession"],"answer":"ло́жная диле́мма — a false choice","why":"It offers only two doors when more exist — a ло́жная диле́мма. Name it calmly and step back onto your ground.","hint":"Count the options it pretends you have — is that really all there are? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which hedge deliberately leaves the possibility open, committing to neither yes nor no?","opts":["несомне́нно","не исключено́","вряд ли"],"answer":"не исключено́","why":"«Не исключено́» — «it can't be ruled out» — leaves the door ajar. «Несомне́нно» is certainty; «вряд ли» leans to no.","hint":"One shuts the door, one leans it toward «no», one leaves it ajar — which is the ajar one? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In «В ко́мнату вошёл Ива́н» (answering «Кто вошёл?»), the new information — the ре́ма — is —","opts":["Ива́н","в ко́мнату","вошёл"],"answer":"Ива́н","why":"The new part lands last, in the strongest position: «Ива́н» is the ре́ма. Word order is how Russian points the spotlight.","hint":"The question is «who came in?» — so which word is the fresh answer, and where does it sit? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"A greeting-card verse or a gift-shop cupid, delivered with a wink as if it were profound, is best judged —","opts":["гру́бость — crude rudeness","по́шлость — smug triteness posing as taste","у́даль — reckless, joyous daring"],"answer":"по́шлость — smug triteness posing as taste","why":"По́шлость fuses «vulgar» and «trite» into one verdict on soul and taste. Rudeness is гру́бость; neither English word alone fits.","hint":"It isn't crude and it isn't merely dull — it's the self-satisfied kind that mistakes itself for refinement 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он настоя́щий интеллиге́нт» describes someone who is —","opts":["clever — quick at solving problems","a first-year university student","of the cultured, conscientious, educated class"],"answer":"of the cultured, conscientious, educated class","why":"False friend: интеллиге́нт names the conscientious, educated type — not «intelligent». For «clever» you'd say у́мный.","hint":"It looks like «intelligent» but points at a whole social-moral type, not raw brainpower 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Beginning each part with the same word — «Жду у́тром, жду днём, жду но́чью» — is the figure called ___. <i>(one Russian noun)</i>","accept":["анафора"],"model":"ана́фора","hint":"The repeated launch — «жду… жду… жду…» — hammers the thought home 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"The emotional and logical peak a speech climbs toward — the one line the room carries out the door — is the ___. <i>(one Russian noun)</i>","accept":["кульминация"],"model":"кульмина́ция","hint":"The high point of the arc, between the arguments and the close — where the register lifts 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"«Дава́йте спо́рить по ___, а не о ли́чностях» — argue on the merits, against the claim itself. <i>(one Russian noun, in the dative)</i>","accept":["существу"],"model":"существу́","hint":"The opposite of «перехо́д на ли́чности» — attack the те́зис, leave the person alone 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"«Он ___ всё сде́лал сам» — the speaker reports the claim and quietly refuses to vouch for it. Fill the one-word particle. <i>(supposedly / allegedly)</i>","accept":["якобы"],"model":"я́кобы","hint":"It smuggles in the speaker's doubt — «supposedly, but I don't buy it» 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"We don't just read — we keep and pass on.","accept":["мы не просто читаем — мы храним и передаём","мы не просто читаем, мы храним и передаём","мы не просто читаем — мы храним и передаем","мы не просто читаем — мы храним, передаём","мы не просто читаем — мы храним и передаём."],"model":"Мы не про́сто чита́ем — мы храни́м и передаём.","hint":"«just» = про́сто; the register lifts at the peak — the verbs mount toward «передаём» 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"Granted, it's expensive, but a mistake will cost more.","accept":["допустим, это дорого, но ошибка обойдётся дороже","допустим это дорого но ошибка обойдётся дороже","допустим, это дорого, но ошибка обойдется дороже","допустим, дорого, но ошибка обойдётся дороже","не спорю, это дорого, но ошибка обойдётся дороже"],"model":"Допу́стим, э́то до́рого, но оши́бка обойдётся доро́же.","hint":"Concede first — «Допу́стим…» — then pivot on «но». Concession earns the counter its force 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"In all likelihood, the decision has already been made.","accept":["по всей видимости, решение уже принято","по всей видимости решение уже принято","по всей видимости, решение принято","по-видимому, решение уже принято","по всей видимости, решение уже принято."],"model":"По всей ви́димости, реше́ние уже́ при́нято.","hint":"The evidential hedge «по всей ви́димости» — nearly sure, read off the visible evidence 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Дли́нное — не зна́чит запу́танное.","accept":["long doesn't mean tangled","long does not mean tangled","being long doesn't mean being tangled","long doesn't mean confusing","long doesn't mean muddled","long isn't the same as tangled"],"model":"Long doesn't mean tangled.","hint":"«дли́нное» = long; «запу́танное» = tangled/snarled — the day's motto for the пери́од 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Он живёт по со́вести.","accept":["he lives by his conscience","he lives according to his conscience","he lives by conscience","he lives by his own conscience","he lives in accordance with his conscience"],"model":"He lives by his conscience.","hint":"«со́весть» = conscience, the inner voice you answer to; «жить по со́вести» = to live by it 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Build a mounting gradation (each verb heavier than the last): \"don't beg, don't demand, don't implore.\"","answer":"не проси не требуй не умоляй","accept":["не проси не требуй не умоляй","не проси, не требуй, не умоляй"],"hint":"Order the three so the heaviest verb («умоля́й») lands last — that's what makes града́ция climb 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble a small period — parallel «когда́» clauses mount the tension, then the main clause resolves: \"When the city falls silent, when the windows go dark, then he sits down to write.\"","answer":"когда смолкает город когда гаснут окна тогда он садится писать","accept":["когда смолкает город когда гаснут окна тогда он садится писать","когда смолкает город, когда гаснут окна, тогда он садится писать","когда смолкает город, когда гаснут окна, — тогда он садится писать"],"hint":"The two «когда́» clauses are the run-up; «тогда́ он сади́тся писа́ть» is the resolution that lands last 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each move in a debate to what it actually is.","pairs":[["усту́пка","concession — grant the strong point, then pivot"],["перехо́д на ли́чности","going personal — the mark of a lost argument"],["подме́на те́зиса","straw man — answering a claim that was never made"],["по существу́","on the merits — attack the claim, not the person"]],"hint":"One grants, one attacks the person, one swaps the claim, one stays on the argument 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each tool from week three to what it does.","pairs":[["града́ция","a mounting series — intensity climbing to the last word"],["не исключено́","a calibrated hedge — the door left ajar"],["пери́од","a long sentence held as an arch — clauses mount, the main resolves"],["тоска́","an aching, objectless longing — unpack it, don't translate"]],"hint":"A figure, a hedge, a sentence-shape, and an untranslatable word — one from each corner of the week 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"Unpack the untranslatable word «по́шлость» in Russian, in 3–4 sentences. Say what it is NOT (the near-words that flatten it — гру́бость, ба́нальность), what it actually holds (self-satisfied triteness passing itself off as taste — a verdict on soul and taste at once), and one situation where only this word fits. Do NOT give a one-word English translation — unfold it.","keywords":["пошлость","грубость","банальность","вкус","самодовольство"],"model":"«По́шлость» — э́то не гру́бость и не про́сто ба́нальность. Э́то самодово́льная безвку́сица, вы́данная за то́нкий вкус: пошля́к уве́рен, что он то́нок и глубо́к. Так звучи́т откры́тка с ле́бедями и́ли тост про «гла́вное — здоро́вье». Одни́м англи́йским сло́вом э́то не переда́ть — сло́во прихо́дится развора́чивать.","minWords":25,"hint":"Name the false friends first — it's not гру́бость, not mere ба́нальность — then the verdict it delivers on both taste and soul 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"Write a short paragraph (4–6 sentences) that USES at least four of week three's skills in service of one thought: a rhetorical figure (ана́фора or града́ция), a пери́од with the payload at the very end, a calibrated hedge (вряд ли / не исключено́ / по всей ви́димости), and a graceful concession (допу́стим… / согла́сен, но…). If it fits, leave one untranslatable word (тоска́ / по́шлость / аво́сь) unpacked, not translated. Theme is free. Then name which skill each sentence used.","keywords":["когда","вряд ли","допустим","не исключено","тоска"],"model":"Когда́ я начина́ла, когда́ я пу́тала падежи́, когда́ ка́ждое сло́во дава́лось с бо́ем, — я вряд ли ве́рила, что дойду́ сюда́. Допу́стим, я и сейча́с зна́ю не всё, — но, по всей ви́димости, гла́вное уже́ случи́лось: ру́сский стал не предме́том, а го́лосом. И да́же «тоску́» я тепе́рь не перевожу́ — я её чу́вствую. (Ана́фора — три «когда́»; пери́од с гла́вным в конце́; хедж «вряд ли / по всей ви́димости»; усту́пка «допу́стим»; безэквивале́нтное сло́во «тоска́».)","minWords":30,"hint":"Don't cram them in mechanically — let one thought carry them. Build to a climax, hedge where you're honestly unsure, concede before you turn, and land the period's last word hard 🙂"}]},"4":{"week":4,"final":true,"title":"Final Exam · Russian C2","intro":"A month ago you could choose the appropriate word; now you hunt the one exact word — and read, write, edit and translate at the very top of the language. These thirty-three questions gather the whole level, with the weight on week four: the classics unadapted and verse, the scholarly article, the editor's пра́вка, translation and its false friends, the essay with a voice, and the whole vertical range of Russian — all resting on the exact word, connotation, subtext, irony, allusion, rhetoric and hedging you built in weeks one to three. Score 80% or more and C2 — the last level — is yours. Take your time; this is the final page of the whole road. 💛","review":["Reading the classics unadapted — recognise the flavour of an archaism and glide on. Tell a <b>dead</b> archaism (<b>сей, дабы́, о́чи, уста́, чело́</b>) from a still-living bookish word (<b>ве́сьма, и́бо, дитя́</b>); an archaism carries solemnity — or, dropped in on purpose, irony.","Reading verse — hear <b>ямб</b> (da-DUM) from <b>хоре́й</b> (DUM-da), name the rhyme (<b>перекрёстная</b> а-б-а-б, <b>па́рная</b> а-а-б-б, <b>кольцева́я</b> а-б-б-а), and accept poetic licence: inversion (<b>Петра́ творе́нье</b>) and поэти́змы (<b>брег, зла́то, глас</b>).","The scholarly article — build it by its parts (<b>аннота́ция → введе́ние → ме́тод → результа́ты → вы́воды</b>), hide the author (<b>в рабо́те пока́зано, что…</b>, not «я счита́ю»), and repeat the exact <b>те́рмин</b> instead of varying it.","The editor's hand — a sentence can be correct and still bad. Cut <b>канцеляри́т</b> (<b>в це́лях улучше́ния → что́бы улу́чшить</b>), <b>плеона́зм</b> (<b>ме́сяц март → март</b>) and <b>тавтоло́гию</b> (<b>спроси́ть вопро́с → зада́ть вопро́с</b>), and un-stack the genitives.","Rendering it faithfully — <b>переда́ть</b> the meaning, don't <b>подста́вить</b> words; keep the tone and irony alive; and watch the bookish false friends (<b>претендова́ть</b> ≠ pretend, <b>актуа́льный</b> ≠ actual, <b>симпати́чный</b> ≠ sympathetic).","The essay with a voice — hold one base register and <b>modulate on purpose</b> (модуля́ция, not a сбо́й); open with a <b>зачи́н</b> that hooks, close with a <b>концо́вка</b> that resonates.","The whole vertical of Russian — understand every floor, from <b>высо́кое</b> down to <b>просторе́чие</b> (<b>ло́жить, и́хний</b>) and <b>жарго́н / сленг / диале́кт</b>, and reach down only on purpose: знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать.","Integration & licence — a native composes, never assembles; run the self-edit (<b>саморедакту́ра</b>) that catches the calque (<b>сде́лать → оказа́ть влия́ние</b>), and earn <b>во́льность</b> — a rule broken on purpose is a приём, not an оши́бка.","Weeks 1–3 underneath — the <b>exact</b> word by its shade (<b>идти́ / шага́ть / плести́сь</b>), connotation (<b>упо́рный</b> vs <b>упря́мый</b>), idiom and allusion without calque (<b>а воз и ны́не там</b>), rhetoric, the calibrated hedge (<b>не исключено́, что…</b>) and the untranslatable (<b>тоска́, по́шлость</b>)."],"q":[{"type":"choice","q":"Editing a modern report, you want «very» in an elevated but still <b>current</b> register. Which word is bookish yet alive today — not a dead archaism?","opts":["ве́сьма","зело́","вельми́"],"answer":"ве́сьма","why":"ве́сьма (= о́чень) is bookish but perfectly current; зело́ and вельми́ are dead archaisms — museum Russian for «very».","hint":"One of these is still written in reports today; the other two you'd meet only in an old chronicle 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In a solemn nineteenth-century line, «Из его́ … излива́лась мольба́» (a prayer poured from his …). Which fits the high, poetic style?","opts":["рот","хле́бало","уста́"],"answer":"уста́","why":"уста́ (= рот, гу́бы) is the lifted, poetic mouth — the one that prays or speaks truth; рот is neutral (it flattens the tone), хле́бало is crude slang (it crashes it).","hint":"The mouth that prays needs the solemn word, not the everyday one and not the crude one 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Бу́ря мгло́ю не́бо кро́ет» (Пу́шкин) — the stress lands on the odd beats, DUM-da. The meter is —","opts":["хоре́й","ямб","верли́бр"],"answer":"хоре́й","why":"Odd-beat stress, DUM-da → хоре́й (trochee); ямб is da-DUM, and верли́бр (free verse) has no fixed beat at all.","hint":"Tap it out — the stress hits first, then falls: DUM-da 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In «Люблю́ тебя́, Петра́ творе́нье» (Пу́шкин, «Ме́дный вса́дник»), «Петра́ творе́нье» for «творе́нье Петра́» is —","opts":["a grammatical mistake to fix","poetic inversion, chosen for meter and grandeur","a printer's error"],"answer":"poetic inversion, chosen for meter and grandeur","why":"Inversion is поэти́ческая во́льность — fronting «Петра́» lifts the tone and fits the beat; un-inverting it flattens the line.","hint":"Don't «correct» verse — the flipped order was chosen on purpose 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In a Russian <b>нау́чная статья́</b>, «I have proven that the result holds» is best written —","opts":["Я доказа́л, что результа́т ве́рен","В рабо́те пока́зано, что результа́т ве́рен","Ща докажу́, что всё норм"],"answer":"В рабо́те пока́зано, что результа́т ве́рен","why":"Нау́чный style hides the author: «в рабо́те пока́зано, что…». «Я доказа́л» imports English rhetoric; «ща… норм» is chat in the wrong universe entirely.","hint":"The scholarly voice takes the author out of it — impersonal, not «I» 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Your editor's pen hits «в це́лях повыше́ния ка́чества». In living Russian it becomes —","opts":["в направле́нии повыше́ния ка́чества","по вопро́су повыше́ния ка́чества","что́бы повы́сить ка́чество"],"answer":"что́бы повы́сить ка́чество","why":"Free the verb: «что́бы повы́сить ка́чество». The other two just swap one bureaucratic turn (канцеляри́т) for another.","hint":"Cure канцеляри́т by putting the verb back where a noun-cluster hides it 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase is a <b>плеона́зм</b> an editor would trim to a single word?","opts":["ка́рие глаза́","свобо́дная вака́нсия","проливно́й до́ждь"],"answer":"свобо́дная вака́нсия","why":"A вака́нсия is a free post by definition, so «свобо́дная» is dead weight (плеона́зм). «ка́рие глаза́» and «проливно́й до́ждь» are correct, fixed collocations.","hint":"Two of these are natural fixed pairs; one word secretly repeats what its partner already says 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́тот вопро́с сейча́с о́чень …» — “this question is very topical right now.” Which word is exact?","opts":["действи́тельный","факти́ческий","актуа́льный"],"answer":"актуа́льный","why":"актуа́льный = topical / relevant / current — the false friend; действи́тельный and факти́ческий both mean «actual, real».","hint":"The English look-alike «actual» is the trap here — reach past it 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which line is <b>просторе́чие</b> — a marker to recognise but never adopt as merely «casual» Russian?","opts":["Куда́ поста́вить кни́ги?","Куда́ ло́жить кни́ги?","Куда́ положи́ть кни́ги?"],"answer":"Куда́ ло́жить кни́ги?","why":"«ло́жить» is substandard; the norm is класть / положи́ть. An educated native hears «ло́жить» as a social marker, not as informality.","hint":"One verb here is not «relaxed» — it's outside the standard 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"In a steadily bookish essay you drop one colloquial word on purpose, then come straight back to your base register. That controlled departure is —","opts":["модуля́ция — a departure you meant","стилево́й сбо́й — a slip of register","канцеляри́т — bureaucratese"],"answer":"модуля́ция — a departure you meant","why":"A controlled step away that returns home is модуля́ция; the same word by accident, over a wobbly base, would read as a сбо́й (slip).","hint":"A note bent on purpose and returned to pitch — not a wrong note 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"A master drops a case ending or a «wrong» word on purpose — for punch, or for irony. Set beside a beginner's slip, this is —","opts":["the same thing, just by a better writer","still an оши́бка (a mistake)","a приём (a device) — a rule broken with intent"],"answer":"a приём (a device) — a rule broken with intent","why":"Intention is the whole difference: a beginner breaks a rule and errs (оши́бка); a master breaks it and means it (приём) — that is во́льность.","hint":"The difference between a slip and a device is one thing: did you mean it? 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"An exhausted man drags himself home, barely lifting his feet. The <b>exact</b> verb for that walk is —","opts":["плести́сь","шага́ть","семени́ть"],"answer":"плести́сь","why":"плести́сь = to trudge, to drag one's feet; шага́ть strides briskly, семени́ть takes tiny quick steps — same register, different shade.","hint":"All three are «to walk» — pick the one that drags 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"Both words describe someone who won't back down. Which one smuggles in <b>approval</b> — persistent, in a good way?","opts":["упря́мый","упо́рный","своенра́вный"],"answer":"упо́рный","why":"упо́рный carries approval (persevering); упря́мый (stubborn) and своенра́вный (wilful) carry the negative verdict — same fact, opposite коннота́ция.","hint":"Same behaviour, but only one of these words pats the person on the back 🙂"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Дава́й … ча́сик и пойдём» — “let's sit a cozy little while, then go.” Which verb carries that bounded, brief-and-pleasant sense?","opts":["посиде́ть","сиде́ть","заси́живаться"],"answer":"посиде́ть","why":"The delimitative <b>по-</b> (посиде́ть) means «to sit for a bounded, cozy little while»; сиде́ть is unbounded, заси́живаться is to overstay.","hint":"The prefix по- tucks the action into a small, warm span of time 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Edit the tautology «спроси́ть вопро́с»: a question is posed, so the native verb is «___ вопро́с».","accept":["задать","задавать"],"model":"Не «спроси́ть вопро́с», а «зада́ть вопро́с».","hint":"Same-root doubling (тавтоло́гия); the verb that pairs with «вопро́с» means «to pose» 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Complete the impersonal нау́чный formula: «В рабо́те ___, что вы́бор сло́ва зави́сит от конте́кста» (it is shown).","accept":["показано"],"model":"В рабо́те пока́зано, что вы́бор сло́ва зави́сит от конте́кста.","hint":"The impersonal passive that keeps the author out — «it is shown» 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Self-editing catches a calque: not «сде́лать оши́бку» (from English «made a mistake») but «___ оши́бку» — the native light verb.","accept":["допустить","допускать","совершить"],"model":"Она́ допусти́ла оши́бку в перево́де.","hint":"Russian «commits / allows» a mistake — it doesn't «make» one 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"A classic writes «В ___ мину́ту он всё по́нял» — the archaic feminine form of «э́ту» (this). Type it (a «сей»-form).","accept":["сию"],"model":"В сию́ мину́ту он всё по́нял.","hint":"The old «сей» in «в … мину́ту» — the set phrase means «this very instant» 🙂"},{"type":"gap","q":"Lines rhyme а-б-а-б — 1 with 3, 2 with 4. This cross-rhyme scheme is «___ ри́фма» (type the adjective).","accept":["перекрестная","перекрёстная"],"model":"Схе́ма а-б-а-б — э́то перекрёстная ри́фма.","hint":"«Cross» rhyme — the commonest scheme, the one «Оне́гин» rides 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"This article examines the problem of translating culture-bound vocabulary.","accept":["в настоящей статье рассматривается проблема перевода безэквивалентной лексики","в данной статье рассматривается проблема перевода безэквивалентной лексики","в статье рассматривается проблема перевода безэквивалентной лексики"],"model":"В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики.","hint":"Open impersonally with the set formula «В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается…» 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"To improve the quality, we repaired the premises.","accept":["чтобы улучшить качество мы отремонтировали помещение","чтобы улучшить качество, мы отремонтировали помещение"],"model":"Что́бы улу́чшить ка́чество, мы отремонти́ровали помеще́ние.","hint":"Living Russian prefers the verb — «что́бы улу́чшить», not the канцеляри́т «в це́лях улучше́ния» 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"He pretended that everything was fine.","accept":["он притворялся что всё в порядке","он притворялся, что всё в порядке","он делал вид что всё в порядке","он делал вид, что всё в порядке","он притворился что всё в порядке","он притворился, что всё в порядке"],"model":"Он притворя́лся, что всё в поря́дке.","hint":"«pretend» is NOT «претендова́ть» (to lay claim) — reach for «притворя́ться» / «де́лать вид» 🙂"},{"type":"trRE","ru":"Allow me to disagree: talent by itself decides nothing.","accept":["позволю себе не согласиться талант сам по себе ничего не решает","позволю себе не согласиться: талант сам по себе ничего не решает","позволю себе не согласиться, талант сам по себе ничего не решает"],"model":"Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться: тала́нт сам по себе́ ничего́ не реша́ет.","hint":"Disagree politely with the set formula «Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться…» 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Ремо́нт обеща́ют тре́тий год, а воз и ны́не там.","accept":["they've been promising the repairs for three years but nothing has moved","they have been promising the repairs for three years, but nothing has moved","the repairs have been promised for three years and nothing has changed","they've been promising to fix it for three years, but nothing has budged","they've promised the repairs for three years but nothing has moved"],"model":"They've been promising the repairs for three years, but nothing has moved.","hint":"«А воз и ны́не там» is a Крыло́в line — render its meaning (nothing has budged), not the cart 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"Ну спаси́бо, удружи́л — тако́го я не ожида́л.","accept":["well thanks a lot, some favour that was — i didn't expect that","oh great, thanks a lot — i wasn't expecting that","well thanks, you've really done me a favour (sarcastically) — i didn't expect that","thanks a bunch, real nice of you — i didn't see that coming","well, thanks a lot — i really wasn't expecting that"],"model":"Well, thanks a lot — I really wasn't expecting that (with a sarcastic sting).","hint":"«спаси́бо, удружи́л» is ironic (антифра́зис) — it means the opposite of gratitude; keep the sting 🙂"},{"type":"trER","en":"В его́ шу́тке была́ кака́я-то по́шлость.","accept":["there was a kind of smug vulgarity to his joke","his joke had a certain tasteless, self-satisfied banality to it","there was something smug and tasteless about his joke","his joke had a smug, trite vulgarity to it","there was a self-satisfied poor taste to his joke"],"model":"There was a smug, tasteless banality to his joke.","hint":"«по́шлость» has no one-word English twin — unpack it: banal, self-satisfied and in poor taste all at once 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble the article's conclusion: “Thus, one may conclude that further research is needed.”","answer":"таким образом можно сделать вывод о необходимости дальнейшего исследования","accept":["таким образом можно сделать вывод о необходимости дальнейшего исследования","таким образом, можно сделать вывод о необходимости дальнейшего исследования"],"hint":"Start with the closing formula «Таки́м о́бразом…» 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Build the archaic purpose clause: “so that I would know” — old style, with «дабы́».","answer":"дабы я знал","accept":["дабы я знал"],"hint":"The museum-piece conjunction standing in for «что́бы» is «дабы́» 🙂"},{"type":"order","hintRu":"Assemble the calibrated hedge: “It is not out of the question that he is right.”","answer":"не исключено что он прав","accept":["не исключено что он прав","не исключено, что он прав"],"hint":"Reach for the mid-scale hedge «не исключено́, что…» 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each word to its layer in the vertical of Russian.","pairs":[["взира́ть","lofty / bookish (высо́кое)"],["смотре́ть","neutral (нейтра́льное)"],["гляде́ть","colloquial but standard (разгово́рное)"],["и́хний","substandard (просторе́чие)"],["ба́ско","regional / dialect (диале́кт)"]],"hint":"Sort the floors: lofty → neutral → colloquial → substandard → regional 🙂"},{"type":"match","q":"Match each bookish false friend to its REAL meaning.","pairs":[["претендова́ть на","to lay claim to / aspire to (NOT to pretend)"],["актуа́льный","topical, current (NOT actual)"],["симпати́чный","likeable, nice-looking (NOT sympathetic)"],["патети́ческий","lofty, full of pathos (NOT pathetic)"],["корре́ктный","polite, proper (NOT correct / right)"]],"hint":"They look like English words but land somewhere else — give the real sense 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"Write a short C2 <b>essay opening</b> (3–5 Russian sentences) on «Что зна́чит владе́ть языко́м в соверше́нстве?». Land a strong <b>зачи́н</b> — a paradox or a sharp claim, never «В настоя́щем эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть…»; hold one bookish-publicistic base register; and modulate it once <b>on purpose</b> (a rhetorical question or an ironic aside). Make it read as a person, not a checklist.","keywords":["владеть","совершенство","оттенок","на мой взгляд","впрочем","язык"],"model":"Владе́ть языко́м в соверше́нстве — во́все не зна́чит не де́лать оши́бок. Ра́зве не свобо́днее тот, кто наруша́ет пра́вило наро́чно? На мой взгля́д, соверше́нство — э́то слух на отте́нок: спосо́бность вы́брать не про́сто ве́рное, а еди́нственно то́чное сло́во. Впро́чем, и э́того ма́ло — ну́жен ещё свой го́лос.","minWords":25,"hint":"Skeleton: a hook (paradox / rhetorical question) → your claim «На мой взгля́д…» → one deliberate modulation. Skip the faceless «я хочу́ рассказа́ть» opener 🙂"},{"type":"describe","prompt":"You are the editor. Rewrite this <b>канцеляри́т</b> into living Russian (2–3 sentences), keeping the meaning: «В це́лях улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания населе́ния в ме́сяце ма́рте была́ произведена́ прове́рка рабо́ты столо́вой». Put the verbs back, cut the плеона́зм («ме́сяц март»), and un-stack the genitives.","keywords":["чтобы","улучшить","в марте","проверили","столовой","обслуживание"],"model":"Что́бы улу́чшить обслу́живание, в ма́рте прове́рили рабо́ту столо́вой.","minWords":12,"hint":"«в це́лях улучше́ния» → «что́бы улу́чшить»; «в ме́сяце ма́рте» → «в ма́рте»; let verbs carry the genitive chain 🙂"}]}};
